From 0388069d3d51495bd985b4e7a59e1ba77d094996 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Danylo Storozhev <office@storozhev.net>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 11:06:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 17/17] [Parser] Recover headless DeepSeek V4.1 tool calls by
 inferring the tool from parameters
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Extends the tool-call healing to the case where the model drops the invoke
opener AND the tool name entirely, emitting only the parameter tags before
`</｜DSML｜ invoke>`. With no name to key on, the earlier healing left the block
as content; here the parameters identify the call.

Engine: add an opt-in headless recovery path (StreamingParserEngine). When a
provisional recovery's captured name is not a tool, instead of aborting, the
buffered opener is reinterpreted as the first parameter, the mistaken TOOL_NAME
events are dropped, and the hold continues in TOOL_ARGS with a deferred name.
Before committing at `</invoke>` the engine consults a parser-supplied inferrer
on the buffered text; if it cannot name a tool, the block is restored as text
rather than committed as a nameless call that would silently vanish. Both hooks
default off, so every other parser is unaffected.

Parser: infer the tool as the single declared tool whose properties cover all
parsed parameter names. A tool always covers its own parameters, so a unique
cover is the right tool and anything ambiguous defers (stays text) — this also
makes partial coverage safe while streaming: a common-only prefix such as
`{"i"}` stays ambiguous until a distinctive parameter arrives, and any unique
early match is already correct. Suppressed when tool_choice is "none".

Verified against real captured headless leaks (a `bash` run and process-manager
calls): recovered with the correct tool and valid arguments, streaming and
non-streaming; unidentifiable blocks restored as text; wrapper-omitted
well-formed invokes and correct DSML unchanged.
